// Koru Standard Library: the `run` part of bridge.kz.
// Joined via `~part run` in bridge.kz; merged into the bridge module at load.

// ============================================================================
// RUN - Execute a turn against the session's resources
// ============================================================================
//
// The verb the bridge existed without: `std/runtime:run` takes a `handle_pool`
// and a `scope`, and a bridge is exactly that pairing outliving a single turn.
//
// The branch set MIRRORS `std/runtime:run` plus TWO bridge-only branches.
// `?partial` is where the mirror ends: sequencing is the agent channel's
// demand (frag-partial-programs), not `runtime:run`'s contract, which stays
// single (orisha's endpoint depends on it). A multi-item turn reports here,
// always, even fully green — one shape for "a turn ran steps".
// `residue` is the other: a turn whose items were all prose binds every
// refused text as a session value and hands the turn back to the agent —
// the callback the harness owes a reply that carried no invocation.
//
// Two of that tor's parameters are deliberately not forwarded:
//
// - `budget` — metering is PARKED. It earns its keep on a public-facing API.
// - `auto_discharge` — always false, and it must be. A bridge-managed pool
//   outlives the run BY DESIGN; `close` is what releases.

// Two required arms: `| result` when the turn completes, `| error` when it
// fails — the RunError union carries every failure class, `kind` naming the
// variant (`error-decode` routes it back to the typed arms). The findings
// stay armable as optionals — the refusal surface is charged once, not
// nine ritual times.

~pub tor run { br: *Bridge<session>, source: string<*> }
| result { value: @import("root").koru_std.koru_interpreter.Value, used: u64, handles: u32, bound: string, trace: string }
| error RunError
| ?defined string
| ?unhandled-branch { branch: string, payload: string, handles: string, bound: string, guides: string }
| ?residue { report: string, observer: bool, bound: string, text: string }
| ?partial { ran: u32, total: u32, used: u64, handles: u32, report: string, wire_failures: u32 }

